  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAnother quiet and muggy morning across the area, with some\npatchy fog that is slowly expanding in coverage. Given rain\nacross much of the area yesterday, fairly light low-level flow,\nand a good amount of clearing through dawn, patchy fog is\npossible across much of the CWA by sunrise, with coverage \ngreatest across the typical valleys or areas that received \nheavier rain yesterday. Fog could be dense in some areas.\n\nActive weather continues across the region today with additional\ndiurnal showers and storms expected this afternoon and evening.\nThe main feature of impact locally has been an upper level low \nthat has been gradually pulled northward, opening up into an\nelongated wave over the past 24 hours, and still overhead of \nthe area per GOES water vapor satellite imagery. This feature \nwill continue to be absorbed into the broader scale upper air \npattern as a longwave trough across the north-central CONUS \ngradually progesses eastward. Forcing with this feature today \nshould be less than that of yesterday, translating into ISOLD to\nSCT showers and storms across the CWA during peak heating. \nOverall, shower and storm chances will be greatest in/near the \nhigher terrain (40-70%), with activity more of the isolated to \nwidely scattered variety further north and west. The pattern \nthen remains active Friday through early next week courtesy of a\nfairly zonal/progressive upper air pattern with periodic weak \ncrossing shortwaves. These waves atop the persistent very warm \nand moist low-level environment will result in continued daily \nshower and storm chances. Rain chances will be favored during \nthe afternoon/evening hours with diurnal heating, but could also\noccur some nights depending on the timing of the aforementioned\nupper level disturbances. A drier interlude could occur sometime\nSunday into Monday as weak upper ridging attempts to build in, \nwith a return to more active weather thereafter.\n\nThe primary point of concern during this timeframe will be the \npotential for daily heavy downpours with diurnal thunderstorm \nactivity given progged PWATs of 1.5-2.0\" by and large through \nearly next week. Given such, localized flash flooding will \nremain possible each afternoon/evening, especially in areas \nthat have recently received heavy rainfall. WPC maintains a \nMarginal Risk for excessive rainfall across much of the area \nthrough the weekend. The potential for severe weather over the \nnext few days continues to remain on the lower side. Even so, \ndaily modest instability and forcing will support at least some \nisolated strong storm potential, with strong wind gusts being \nthe threat if any heavier storms were to develop. \n\nTemperatures are progged to be slightly above normal throughout\nthe period, with highs generally in the mid to upper 80s across\nthe lowlands, amid high humidity. A robust shortwave diving \nsouthward out of Canada will attempt to provide some relief \ntoward mid next week with the passage of a cold front, but\nguidance varies on when/if the front makes it south through the\nCWA.",
